.TH PLAYBOOK 1 "2026-08-14" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
playbook \- carry a project's standing brief through its issue queue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B playbook
push <owner/repo> <file>
.br
.B playbook
pull <owner/repo> [outfile]
.br
.B playbook
check <owner/repo>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The standing brief a dispatch run reads first, before it touches the queue.
It lives in exactly ONE open GitHub issue per repo, labelled
.BR playbook .
Two such issues is the failure this guards against: two briefs that
disagree, with the reader seeing whichever one
.B gh
listed first.
.PP
.B push
upserts the brief from a file -- it edits the existing issue rather than
opening a second one, and refuses an empty file.
.B pull
fetches the brief, to stdout or to
.IR outfile .
.B check
reports the issue number, last-updated time and author.
.B pull
and
.B check
both REFUSE if zero or more than one open issue carries the label, rather
than guessing.
.PP
Needs only
.BR gh (1)
on
.BR PATH ,
so
.B pull
works from any account with a token and no repo write access at all.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 ok; 2 usage; 4 GAP (unrecognised subcommand); 5 BROKEN (no brief, more
than one brief, or an empty one); 6 BLIND (\fBgh\fR is not on PATH).
.SH FLAGS
Global flags (\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR, ...) go
AFTER the subcommand, e.g.
.IR "playbook push hf7y/foo brief.md --dry-run" .
A flag before the subcommand is parsed as "no subcommand given", per the
shared bashified dispatch convention.
.PP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
applies to
.B push
only: prints what would be created or updated and writes nothing.
.B \-f, \-\-force
is accepted (this verb changes state) but overrides no refusal here --
none of them is safe to skip silently.
